{"documentId":"tos.work.sextus-empiricus.outlines-of-pyrrhonism","versionId":"retained-outlines-en-forage2026-ai","revision":"5796826c1e9b123b1253409ad3056e735c353f4f4bf94b1bdf84be7251587ff7","units":[{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-ac3723e5144141fab38ef0dfd82a8bf8","ordinal":401,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.160","text":"\n\nWe proceed in the same way with the second indemonstrable. Either it is possible for the antecedent to hold when the consequent does not, or it is not possible. If it is possible, the conditional will not be sound. If it is not possible, then as soon as “not the consequent” is posited, “not the antecedent” is posited too. Once again the conditional is redundant, and the argument is propounded like this: “It is not light; therefore [that] it is not day.”"},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-b6b6c02d419b40689874499787e09767","ordinal":402,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.161","text":"\n\nThe same reasoning applies to the third indemonstrable. Either it is evident that the parts of the conjunction cannot hold together, or it is non-evident. If non-evident, we will not grant the negated conjunction. If evident, then as soon as one is posited the other is eliminated, and the negated conjunction is redundant, since we propound the argument like this: “It is day; therefore it is not night.”"},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-c76d582ce4c24968aede3d6276b9b554","ordinal":403,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.162","text":"\n\nWe say much the same about the fourth and fifth indemonstrables. Either it is evident that in the disjunction one part is true and the other false, in complete conflict, which is what the disjunction announces, or it is non-evident. If non-evident, we will not grant the disjunction. If evident, then when one of them is posited it is plain that the other does not hold, and when one is eliminated it is evident that the other holds. So it suffices to propound the arguments like this: “It is day; therefore it is not night,” “It is not day; therefore it is night”; and the disjunction is redundant."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-145e61d80ce741568760aaa2bea8947a","ordinal":404,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.163","text":"\n\nMuch the same can be said about the so-called categorical syllogisms too, which are used above all by the Peripatetics. Take this argument: “What is just is fine, ⟨and⟩ what is fine is good; therefore what is just is good.” Either it is agreed and evident that what is fine is good, or it is disputed and non-evident. If it is non-evident, it will not be granted when the argument is propounded, and so the syllogism will not be conclusive. But if it is evident that whatever is fine is certainly also good, then as soon as it is said that this particular thing is fine, its being good comes in along with it. So an argument like “What is just is fine; therefore what is just is good” suffices, and the other premise, in which what is fine was said to be good, is redundant."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-f3f63334bb64465b94acab6d6f4f6410","ordinal":405,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.164","text":"\n\nLikewise with an argument like “Socrates is a human being; every human being is an animal; therefore Socrates is an animal.” If it is not immediately evident that whatever is a human being is also an animal, the universal premise is not agreed, and we will not grant it in the"},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-fa056935560641b0a4952313de29ffbc","ordinal":406,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.165","text":"\n\npropounding of the argument. But if being an animal follows from being a human being, and for that reason the premise “every human being is an animal” is admittedly true, then as soon as it is said that Socrates is a human being, his being an animal comes in along with it. So an argument like “Socrates is a human being; therefore Socrates is an animal” suffices, and the premise “every human being is an animal” is redundant."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-7ed50d7312a04a37b0f8bc122da1d20c","ordinal":407,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.166","text":"\n\nSimilar methods can be used with the other primary categorical arguments too, but let us not dwell on them now. The dialecticians lay these arguments down as the foundation of syllogisms, and they are redundant. So, as far as redundancy goes, the whole of dialectic is overturned, since we cannot distinguish the redundant, and therefore inconclusive, arguments from the so-called conclusive syllogisms."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-f57b04e605a2412f80bf57ae426d45c2","ordinal":408,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.167","text":"\n\nAnd if some people do not accept that there are single-premise arguments, they are no more trustworthy than Antipater, who does not reject such arguments either. For these reasons, then, what the dialecticians call a conclusive argument cannot be decided. Moreover, the true argument cannot be found, both for the reasons already given and because it must certainly end in something true. For the conclusion said to be true is either apparent or non-evident.\n〔1〕 Antipater of Tarsus was head of the Stoic school in the second century BC."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-80836cd9bdcf42ef8838f3282aa696ec","ordinal":409,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.168","text":"\n\nIt certainly cannot be apparent. If it were, it would not be revealed through the premises, since it would strike us of itself and would be no less apparent than its premises. If it is non-evident, then, since there is undecidable disagreement about non-evident things, as we pointed out earlier, and for that reason they are inapprehensible, the conclusion of the argument said to be true will be inapprehensible too. And if this is inapprehensible, we will not know whether what is deduced is true or false. So we will not know whether the argument is true or false, and the true argument will not be found."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-42450311b44f43ada9c1a4648a20c2e4","ordinal":410,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.169","text":"\n\nBut let us pass over these points too. The argument that deduces something non-evident through evident things cannot be found. The conclusion follows from the conjunction of its premises; what follows, the consequent, is relative, relative to the antecedent; and relatives are apprehended together with one another, as we showed. So if the conclusion is non-evident, the premises will be non-evident too. And if the premises are evident, the conclusion will be evident too, since it is apprehended together with them, evident as they are. Then something non-evident is no longer deduced from evident things."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-f926feec02b64d81bd5b3858de79daea","ordinal":411,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.170","text":"\n\nFor these reasons the conclusion is not revealed by the premises either. Either it is non-evident and not apprehended, or it is evident and does not need anything to reveal it. Proof is said to be an argument that, by way of deduction (that is, conclusively), reveals a non-evident conclusion through things admittedly true. But we have pointed out that there is no argument, nor any conclusive one, nor any true one, nor any that deduces something non-evident through evident things, nor any that reveals its conclusion. So it is plain that proof has no real existence."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-d1bafb00143c4eaaa2f5499f40091a2d","ordinal":412,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.171","text":"\n\nWe will also find proof to be unreal, or even inconceivable, by the following approach. Whoever says that there is proof posits either generic proof or some specific proof. But it is possible to posit neither generic nor specific proof, as we will point out, and nothing besides these can be conceived. So no one can posit proof as existing."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-2d0e0583e3f64b1dbc0e910d6787cde4","ordinal":413,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.172","text":"\n\nNow, generic proof is unreal, for the following reasons. Either it has some premises and some conclusion, or it does not. If it does not, it is not even a proof. But if it has some premises and some conclusion, then, since everything that is proved and that proves in this way is particular, it will be a specific proof. So there is no generic proof. But there is no specific proof either."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-3c2fbac0a8e840f1bd4264e70a14ceec","ordinal":414,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.173","text":"\n\nFor they will call a proof either the compound made up of the premises and the conclusion, or the compound of the premises alone. But neither of these is a proof, as I will establish. So there is no specific proof."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-0c80a17cc4ba478fabe5d85f79d1c36e","ordinal":415,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.174","text":"\n\nThe compound made up of the premises and the conclusion is not a proof. First, since it has a part that is non-evident, namely the conclusion, it will itself be non-evident, which is absurd. For if the proof is non-evident, it will need something to prove it rather than being able to prove other things."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-ee81a65d7b704a288fc4002c2de4b415","ordinal":416,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.175","text":"\n\nNext, they say that proof is relative, and relative to the conclusion. Relative things, as they themselves say, are thought of in relation to other things. So what is proved must be different from the proof. If, then, the conclusion is what is proved, the proof will not be thought of together with the conclusion. Indeed, either the conclusion contributes something to its own proof or it contributes nothing at all. If it contributes, it will reveal itself. If it does not contribute but is redundant, it will not even be part of the proof, since otherwise we would call the proof itself faulty by redundancy."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-4e7e65abd0e64fac837427d6193ebe4f","ordinal":417,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.176","text":"\n\nNor would the compound of the premises alone be a proof. For who would say that something stated like this, “If it is day, it is light; but it is day” [it is light], is an argument, or even expresses a complete thought at all? So the compound of the premises alone is not a proof either. Therefore specific proof has no reality either. And if neither specific nor generic proof exists, and apart from these no proof can be conceived, proof is unreal."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-73f5bd3e156445e285acae7d9cd508ed","ordinal":418,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.177","text":"\n\nAgain, the unreality of proof can be pointed out from the following. If there is proof, it is either an apparent proof revealing something apparent, or a non-evident proof revealing something non-evident, or a non-evident proof revealing something apparent, or an apparent proof revealing something non-evident. But it cannot be conceived as any of these. So it is inconceivable."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-208738e986da43e9adc186d3caa9d162","ordinal":419,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.178","text":"\n\nIf it is an apparent proof revealing something apparent, then what is revealed will be at once apparent and non-evident. It will be apparent because that is how it was assumed to be. It will be non-evident because it needs something to reveal it and does not present itself to us clearly of its own accord. If it is a non-evident proof of something non-evident, it will itself need something to reveal it and will not reveal other things, which departs from the very concept of proof."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-f83ec27dfd5c463dbff85cb701aecb5d","ordinal":420,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.179","text":"\n\nFor these reasons there cannot be a non-evident proof of something evident either. Nor can there be an evident proof of something non-evident. Proof is relative, and relative things are apprehended together with one another. So what is said to be proved, being apprehended together with the evident proof, will be evident. The argument thus turns itself around, and the proof is not found to be an evident proof of something non-evident. So if proof is neither apparent of the apparent, nor non-evident of the non-evident, nor non-evident of the evident, nor evident of the non-evident, and they say there is nothing besides these, we must say that proof is nothing."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-1ee36fc9519a48e2b14c14b1c61ea7da","ordinal":421,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.180","text":"\n\nIn addition to this, the following should also be said. There is disagreement about proof. Some say that it does not even exist, as do those who claim that nothing exists at all. Others say that it exists, as do most of the dogmatists. And we say that it “no more” exists than does not exist."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-4151d51a21b14446898a94cf100dac87","ordinal":422,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.181","text":"\n\nBesides, a proof always contains a belief, and there has been disagreement about every belief. So there must be disagreement about every proof. Suppose, for the sake of argument, that the proof that there is void is granted. Then the existence of void is granted along with it. Clearly, then, those who dispute whether there is void also dispute its proof. The same argument holds for the other beliefs that proofs are about. So every proof is disputed and in disagreement."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-ef55406203fa4d8685a5797717b06c42","ordinal":423,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.182","text":"\n\nSince, then, proof is non-evident because of the disagreement about it (for things in dispute, insofar as they are disputed, are non-evident), it is not plain of itself but must be established for us by a proof. Now the proof by which proof is established will not be agreed upon and plain, since we are now investigating whether there is any proof at all. Being disputed and non-evident, it will need another proof, and that one another, and so on to infinity. But it is impossible to prove infinitely many things. So it is impossible to establish that there is proof."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-8f8e78234f3b441cbd3fc790feffb38d","ordinal":424,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.183","text":"\n\nNor can it be revealed through a sign. It is under investigation whether there is a sign, and the sign needs a proof for its own reality. So we find the reciprocal mode: proof needs a sign, and the sign in turn needs proof, which is absurd. For these reasons it is not possible to decide the disagreement about proof either. Deciding needs a criterion. But there is an investigation into whether there is a criterion, as we have shown, and so the criterion needs a proof to show that there is some criterion. And so the difficulty of the reciprocal mode turns up again."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-2812a1bc8cb74d0bb92bb9b39eecc1d3","ordinal":425,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.184","text":"\n\nSo it is not possible to point out by proof, or by sign, or by criterion that there is proof, and proof is not evident of itself either, as we have shown. Then whether there is proof will be inapprehensible. For this reason proof will also be unreal. It is conceived together with its proving, but it could not prove anything if it is not apprehended. Hence there will be no proof."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-a2670d18c156473a98e77eec609475b3","ordinal":426,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.185","text":"\n\nThis much, said in outline, will be enough on proof as well. The dogmatists, arguing the opposite, say that the arguments propounded against proof are either demonstrative or not demonstrative. If they are not demonstrative, they cannot show that there is no proof. If they are demonstrative, they themselves bring in the reality of proof, by self-refutation."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-426b826154fd46cab8970c25164bcfc7","ordinal":427,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.186","text":"\n\nHence they also propound an argument like this: “If there is proof, there is proof; if there is no proof, there is proof; but either there is proof or there is no proof; therefore there is proof.” With the same force they also propound this argument: “What follows from opposites is not only true but necessary. These are opposed to each other: ‘There is proof’—‘There is no proof’; and from each of them it follows that there is proof. Therefore there is proof.”"},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-d949f3ee2d944e40a7040d94246e85b6","ordinal":428,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.187","text":"\n\nNow, it is possible to reply to these. For instance, since we do not think that any argument is demonstrative, we do not say outright that the arguments against proof are demonstrative either. We say only that they appear persuasive to us, and persuasive arguments are not necessarily demonstrative. But if they are in fact demonstrative (which we do not affirm), they are surely true as well. True arguments are those that conclude something true by way of true premises. So their conclusion is true. But their conclusion was “So there is no proof.” Therefore “There is no proof” is ⟨true⟩, by self-refutation."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-53720ff5e3054e43bfd707c0b78ccaf9","ordinal":429,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.188","text":"\n\nPurgative drugs flush themselves out along with the matter present in the body. In the same way these arguments can cancel themselves along with the other arguments said to be demonstrative. There is nothing incongruous in this. The phrase “Nothing is true” likewise turns around not only every other statement but itself along with them. Again, this argument can be shown to be inconclusive: “If there is proof, there is proof; if there is no proof, there is proof; but either there is or there is not; therefore there is.” There are several ways of doing so, but for present purposes the following line of reasoning is enough."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-32c5eff0a5d44953a2f8f755ca53d03d","ordinal":430,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.189","text":"\n\nIf this conditional is sound, “If there is proof, there is proof,” then the opposite of its consequent, that is, “There is no proof,” must conflict with “There is proof,” since that is the antecedent of the conditional. But according to them a conditional made up of conflicting propositions cannot be sound. A conditional announces that if its antecedent holds, its consequent holds too. Conflicting propositions announce the opposite: if either one of them holds, whichever it is, the other cannot. So if the conditional “If there is proof, there is proof” is sound, the conditional “If there is no proof, there is proof” cannot be sound."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-1e381761c804496d9fecbe9ca227943a","ordinal":431,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.190","text":"\n\nAgain, suppose we grant for the sake of hypothesis that the conditional “If there is no proof, there is proof” is sound. Then “There is proof” can hold together with “There is no proof.” And if it can hold together with it, it does not conflict with it. So in the conditional “If there is proof, there is proof” the opposite of the consequent does not conflict with the antecedent. So this conditional, in its turn, will not be sound,"},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-a52d37be7bd94768af31d6b18a27baea","ordinal":432,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.191","text":"\n\nsince the other one is laid down as sound by concession, and “There is no proof” does not conflict with “There is proof.” Nor will the disjunction “Either there is proof or there is no proof” be sound. For a sound disjunction announces that one of its members is true and the other, or the others, false, with conflict. Or, if the disjunction is sound, the conditional “If there is no proof, there is proof” is once again found to be faulty, being made up of conflicting propositions. So the premises in the argument just stated are discordant and do away with one another. Hence the argument is not sound."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-6e209144e3984424854e0b62c0d5957f","ordinal":433,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.192","text":"\n\nNor can they show that anything follows from opposites, since they have no criterion of following, as we have argued. But we say all this as a bonus. For suppose the arguments in favor of proof are persuasive (let them be so), and the objections brought against proof are persuasive too. Then we must suspend judgment about proof as well, saying that there is “no more” proof than no proof."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-5c95833d6c464f7584f9e42911ab7414","ordinal":434,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.193","text":"\n\nThat is why it is perhaps superfluous to go through the much-talked-of syllogisms. For one thing, they are turned around along with the reality of proof, since clearly, if there is no proof, there is no room for a demonstrative argument either. For another, we have in effect already argued against them in what we said earlier. When we were discussing redundancy, we described a method by which it can be shown that all the demonstrative arguments of both the Stoics and the Peripatetics turn out to be inconclusive."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-55bc3abc5c464f9f8e55be01e9cc5a49","ordinal":435,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.194","text":"\n\nStill, as an extra, it is perhaps no bad thing to deal with them separately too, since they pride themselves on them above all. Much could be said in pointing out that they are unreal, but in an outline it is enough to use the following method against them. Here too I will speak about the indemonstrables. If these are done away with, all the remaining arguments are overturned as well, since they derive from them the proof that they are conclusive."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-945e15bd060744bb9fd5022cca186b80","ordinal":436,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.195","text":"\n\nNow, the premise “Every human is an animal” is confirmed by induction from the particular cases. Socrates, being human, is also an animal, and likewise Plato and Dion and each of the particular cases. From this it seems possible to affirm that every human is an animal. So if even one of the particular cases should appear to conflict with the others, the universal premise is not sound. For instance, most animals move the lower jaw, and the crocodile alone moves the upper. So the premise “Every animal moves its lower jaw” is not true.\n〔1〕 Dion is the stock name for an arbitrary person in Stoic logic, something like “John Doe.”"},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-94f5af42f60d44b785e587e61dace7bd","ordinal":437,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.196","text":"\n\nSo when they say “Every human is an animal; Socrates is human; therefore Socrates is an animal,” they want to deduce from the universal premise “Every human is an animal” the particular one “Therefore Socrates is an animal.” But this particular premise is just what confirms the universal premise by way of induction, as we pointed out. So they fall into the reciprocal argument, confirming the universal premise by means of each of the particular ones ⟨......⟩"},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-b6c1474f035c46b68dba291354bc32ed","ordinal":438,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.197","text":"\n\nfrom the universal by syllogism. Similarly with an argument like this: “Socrates is human; no human is four-footed; therefore Socrates is not four-footed.” They want to confirm the premise “No human is four-footed” inductively from the particular cases, and they want to deduce each of the particular cases from “No human is four-footed.” So they fall into the difficulty of reciprocity."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-14d8f3a82256416a833778a77589a59d","ordinal":439,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.198","text":"\n\nThe rest of the indemonstrables spoken of among the Peripatetics should be examined in the same way. So should arguments like this one: “If it is day, it is light.” For “If it is day, it is light” is, as they say, what concludes “It is light,” and “It is light,” together with “It is day,” is what confirms “If it is day, it is light.” The conditional just mentioned would not have been thought sound unless “It is light” had first been observed always to hold together with “It is day.”"},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-c1971599fef94e068c24a61abaad6926","ordinal":440,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.199","text":"\n\nSo, in order to frame the conditional “If it is day, it is light,” one must have apprehended beforehand that whenever it is day there is certainly light too. Yet it is by means of this conditional that one concludes that when it is day it is light. As far as the indemonstrable in question goes, the conditional “If it is day, it is light” concludes that its being day and its being light hold together, while their holding together confirms the conditional. So here too the difficulty of the reciprocal mode overturns the reality of the argument."}]}